{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Live root-disk diagnosis, 2026-09-05 09:15-09:55 UTC (read-only SSM probes, host i-00880b2c7f13527c5)\n\nDiagnosis only — no host state was mutated. Everything below marked MEASURED is a direct\nprobe result; anything marked INFERRED is not.\n\n### Headline: root is not on a monotonic climb. It is a ~2 GB SAWTOOTH.\n\nMEASURED `df -k /` series (used KB, then Use%):\n\n```\n09:15:11  42,176,944  68%\n09:17:16  42,176,944  68%\n09:19:54  42,177,348  68%\n09:27:57  42,396,420  68%\n09:33:53  44,359,964  71%   <-- +2.08 GB vs 09:19\n09:36:26  44,158,704  71%\n09:37:26  43,992,476  71%\n09:38:26  42,412,096  68%   <-- -1.58 GB in ONE minute\n09:39:26  42,493,052  68%\n09:40:26  42,926,260  69%\n```\n\nBetween 09:15 and 09:20 root grew **404 KB in 2.6 minutes** — essentially flat. Then it rose\n2.08 GB and gave 1.58 GB straight back. The \"+1.69 pts/hr sustained\" reading is an artifact of\nsampling a sawtooth; the amplitude is ~2 GB (~3.3 points) and the period is tens of minutes.\nThis matches the 08:44 -> 09:14 partial reversal in the 15-minute series.\n\n### The producer: `rebar-autodeploy.timer` build+recreate cycles\n\nMEASURED, `systemctl cat rebar-autodeploy.timer`:\n\n```\nOnBootSec=2min\nOnUnitActiveSec=2min\n```\n\nThe timer polls every 2 minutes and rebuilds whenever `main` advances. MEASURED from\n`journalctl -u rebar-autodeploy`, `main` advanced seven times in the probe window:\n`070c54e6 -> 1f1ecea0 -> a33333b8 -> c0199203 -> c4e43e3f -> cf9c3daf -> 8629e387`, with\n`mcp` or `review-bot` image builds at 06:19, 06:32, 06:33, 07:40, 08:07, 08:30, 09:10, 09:13\n— roughly one build every 20-25 minutes.\n\n### Where the oscillating bytes live: container writable layers in overlay2\n\nMEASURED per-container `GraphDriver.Data.UpperDir` sizes at 09:33:53 (peak of the sawtooth):\n\n| Container | State | Started | UpperDir size |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `rebar-mcp-adfc5285fad6-8092` | running | 09:11:25 | **2,097,656 KB (2.10 GB)** |\n| `rebar-mcp-9d5a7278c892-8093` | **exited** | 08:30:57 | **1,858,500 KB (1.86 GB)** |\n| `compose-gerrit-1` | running | 05:23:32 | 430,956 KB (431 MB) |\n| `compose-review-bot-1` | running | 08:29:47 | 16 KB |\n| `compose-opcert-1` | running | 05:23:32 | 0 KB |\n\nSo **3.96 GB sat in two blue-green MCP writable layers simultaneously**, one of them exited and\nstill resident. The running MCP container accumulated **2.10 GB in ~22 minutes**.\n\nCross-check that this IS the oscillating mass — MEASURED `du -xsk /var/lib/docker/overlay2`:\n\n```\n09:17:16  12,338,996 KB\n09:33:53  13,852,180 KB   (+1.51 GB)\n~09:45    12,167,268 KB   (-1.69 GB)\n```\n\nThe 1.69 GB overlay2 drop matches the 1.58 GB df drop at 09:38. The sawtooth is\n**overlay2 container writable layers**, not build cache and not anything outside docker.\n\nINFERRED (not directly measured): the ~2 GB inside each `rebar-mcp` writable layer is the\ntickets-store clone that `reviewbot-ensure-tickets.sh` performs at container start\n(`git clone --single-branch --branch tickets`). I did not walk the diff dir to confirm its\ncontents.\n\n### Aggravating factor: 21-minute container recreates hold BOTH layers\n\nMEASURED:\n\n```\n08:08:15  Container compose-review-bot-1  Recreate\n08:29:47  Container compose-review-bot-1  Recreated   (21m32s)\n09:13:24  Container compose-review-bot-1  Recreate\n09:34:24  Container compose-review-bot-1  Recreated   (21m00s)\n```\n\nFor 21 minutes on every redeploy the old and new container layers coexist on root. That is the\nrising half of the sawtooth, and the fall is the old container finally being reaped.\n\n### The reclaim is reporting success while freeing nothing\n\nMEASURED `prune_docker_caches` lines from the same journal, in order:\n\n```\n06:13:50  freed=434,236 kB\n06:20:05  freed=4 kB\n06:32:54  freed=310,048 kB\n06:33:33  freed=190,040 kB\n07:40:44  freed=187,076 kB\n08:29:57  freed=564 kB\n08:31:22  freed=348,456 kB\n09:12:39  freed=125,564 kB\n09:34:40  freed=988 kB\n```\n\nMedian ~190 MB against a ~2 GB swing; three of nine runs freed under 1 MB and still logged as a\nnormal outcome. This is the pattern already captured on `taurine-smiling-hamadryad`\n(\"reports success without measuring effect\"), still live.\n\n### The BuildKit budget is being EXCEEDED, not merely pinned\n\nMEASURED `docker system df`:\n\n```\nImages         5   4   3.081GB   957.7MB (31%)\nContainers     4   4   1.369GB   0B (0%)\nLocal Volumes 11   1   988.7MB   1.207MB (0%)\nBuild Cache  104   0   5.875GB   5.875GB\n```\n\n`daemon.json` sets `builder.gc.maxUsedSpace = 5368709120` (5.00 GiB). Build cache is at\n**5.875 GB across 104 entries, 0 active, 100% reclaimable** — i.e. ~0.9 GB OVER budget with GC\nlagging the build cadence. The `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` metric reading \"99-100%\" is\ntherefore under-reporting an overshoot, not showing a cap holding at its limit.\n\n### Ruled out, with numbers\n\n- **Deleted-but-open files** — MEASURED total across every `/proc/*/fd`: `deleted_open_bytes=2,097,152` (2 MB). Not the story.\n- **`/tmp`** — MEASURED as **tmpfs**, 3.8 G size, **100 KB used**. Host-side gate scratch under `/tmp` costs RAM, not root disk. (Container-internal `/tmp` is a different matter — that IS the overlay2 upper dir above.)\n- **`/var/tmp`** — MEASURED 3,751,848 KB (3.75 GB), but 3.75 GB of it is two stale dirs `rebar-review-rehearsal.9eL926` (1.88 GB) and `rebar-review-rehearsal.e3gHec` (1.87 GB), both **mtime 2026-08-27** — nine days old, static. Dead weight, not a producer.\n- **journald** — MEASURED `/var/log/journal` 1,795,632 KB (1.80 GB), `/var/log` total 1.93 GB. Flat, bounded, consistent with the 57% metric.\n- **`/var/gerrit`** — separate volume `nvme1n1`, MEASURED 9.2 G used of 49.9 G (18%). Not root.\n\n### Confirmed: the gate-scratch volume never entered service\n\nMEASURED: `lsblk` shows `nvme2n1  50G` with **no fstype and no mountpoint**, and\n`file -s /dev/nvme2n1` returns `/dev/nvme2n1: data` — raw, unformatted. This independently\nconfirms `renowned-corked-hapuku` and `findable-sure-lynx`. Because host `/tmp` is tmpfs, the\npractical consequence today is that gate scratch lands in **RAM**, not on root — a different\nfailure mode than `1ef8` described, and worth noting since `kswapd0` was MEASURED at 3.4% CPU\nwith `kcompactd0` active, i.e. the host is already under memory pressure.\n\n### An unexplained ~21 GB, which is NOT the grower\n\nMEASURED subtree totals (~09:45):\n\n```\n/var/lib/docker   13,227,224 KB   13.23 GB\n/var/tmp           3,751,848 KB    3.75 GB\n/var/log           1,927,132 KB    1.93 GB\n/usr               1,952,540 KB    1.95 GB\n/var/cache           156,916 KB    0.16 GB\n/opt + /root + /etc + /boot          0.22 GB\n                                 ---------\n                                   21.24 GB\n```\n\n`df` reports **42.4-42.9 GB used** over the same period. **~21 GB is unaccounted.** I could not\nclose this read-only. `du -x /` does not complete — it timed out at 400 s having reached only\n`/local`, `/etc`, `/boot`; the root fs carries **2,343,321 used inodes**.\n\nLeading hypothesis, INFERRED and untested: files **shadowed under mount points** — written to\n`/var/gerrit`, to the nine `/var/lib/docker/volumes/*/_data` bind targets, or to `/tmp` before\ntheir filesystems were mounted over them. Both `du -x` and `find -xdev` stop at a mountpoint, so\nshadowed content is invisible to every read-only probe I have. Confirming it requires a\nmutation (see below).\n\nNote also that `find / -xdev -type f -printf '%b'` sums are **hardlink-inflated** and must not\nbe compared against `du`: the aggregation returned 59.92 GB for `/var/lib` where `du` says\n13.23 GB, a ~4.5x amplification from overlay2 image-layer hardlinks. This is the same\napparent-vs-allocated class of error as `regretful-enormous-horsefly`.\n\n### Is this epic 6202's subject?\n\n**Yes, squarely** — it is Defect 1, \"root-volume consumers grow without a bounded ceiling\", and\nit lands on two children specifically:\n\n- **S5 `francium-written-squid` (in_progress)** — writable container layers. The 3.96 GB\n  blue-green MCP pair is exactly this, and it is the dominant term. Per the S5 finding that\n  `--storage-opt size=` needs XFS `pquota` and a reboot, running layers are unbounded today.\n- **S2 `geode-joking-cockatiel` (closed)** — the BuildKit cap is measurably **over** its 5 GiB\n  budget at 5.875 GB with 0 active entries. Worth reopening or filing as a regression.\n\nI did NOT file a new bug. Nothing here needs one; the mechanism is 6202's and the two specific\ngaps belong on S5 and S2.\n\n### Mutations that would help — NOT run, operator decides\n\n1. **`docker container prune -f`** (or removing the exited `rebar-mcp-9d5a7278c892-8093`).\n   Would reclaim the MEASURED **1.86 GB** exited MCP writable layer. Blast radius: destroys the\n   exited container's filesystem and any forensic evidence in it; removes ALL exited containers,\n   not just this one. Does not touch running containers. Reversible only from image rebuild.\n2. **`docker builder prune -f`.** Would reclaim the MEASURED **5.875 GB** of build cache (0\n   entries active, 100% reclaimable). Blast radius: the next autodeploy build loses all layer\n   caching, so one build runs cold — minutes slower, and during it root rises again. No data\n   loss.\n3. **Removing the two stale `/var/tmp/rebar-review-rehearsal.*` dirs.** Would reclaim a MEASURED\n   **3.75 GB**. Blast radius: nine-day-old rehearsal artifacts; verify nothing references them\n   first. This is the cleanest single reclaim available.\n4. **`mount --bind / /mnt/root-view` (read-only diagnostic).** The only way to see shadowed\n   content and settle the ~21 GB. Blast radius: adds a mount entry; no data is modified;\n   `umount` reverses it. Still a mutation, so I did not run it.\n\nNone of these change the sawtooth. The sawtooth is bounded and self-reclaiming; the real risk is\nthat its ~2 GB peak rides on top of a ~21 GB baseline nobody can currently attribute."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788602258651593679,"uuid":"99b90299-816f-46be-82b9-6fd76ab154d0"}